  "query_preview": "..\n(Tonight through next Wednesday)\n\nWEATHER PATTERN OVERVIEW: Building high pressure establishes \nitself offshore of SoCal today, keeping the main storm track pushed\nto the north and into the PacNW. This keeps fair weather in place\ntoday and Friday under a continued warming trend. By Friday, \ndaytime high temperatures will reside in the upper 40s to upper \n50s, which is around ten degrees warmer than normal for this time \nof year. Though by Friday evening, light rain and snow showers \nwill return to northern Nevada as a shortwave moves into the \nintermountain west from the northwest. The best opportunity for \nshower activity will be within northern Elko County though any \naccumulation looks to remain less than 0.10 inches. Westerly winds\nwill also be gusty Friday evening and into Saturday morning in \nconjunction with the passage of the shortwave, particularly within\nElko County. Gusts as high as 40 to 45 mph are expected. Weather \nwill otherwise be fair Saturday as a transient upper level ridge \nprogresses through the region from the west behind the shortwave. \n\nThe next Pacific storm system moves into the west coast Sunday,\nkicking off a period of active weather for next week as a more \nprogressive upper level pattern sets up across the intermountain \nwest. The first of back to back Pacific systems moving in Sunday\nwill be relatively modest and warm. Snow levels are indicated to \nbe around 7000 feet Sunday night. Light valley rain and mountain \nsnow will be focused across northern Nevada, primarily north of \nthe I-80 corridor. Accumulation also looks modest with valleys \nobserving around 0.10 inches or less. Southwesterly winds will be \nelevated throughout the region. After a brief lull in activity \nMonday, the second, likely stronger Pacific system, is indicated \nto move into the west coast Tuesday. Recent numerical model \nsolutions are coming into much better agreement with regard to \nthe Tuesday event. Widespread valley rain and mountain snow is \nforecast with the greatest accumulation anticipated to be observed\nacross central Nevada based on the indicated trajectory of the \nsystem. Activity clears from northwest to southeast Wednesday. \n\nFORECAST CONFIDENCE & DEVIATIONS: Confidence is high with regard\nto warmer than average temperatures and fair weather thru Friday.\nConfidence is moderate pertaining to increasing probability of\nprecipitaton Friday evening and into Saturday morning. Moderate \nconfidence pertaining to active weather early next week, beginning\nSunday. Minimal deviations from NBM/WPC forecast.",
